		{
			"year": 1800,
			"yearLabel": "c. 1800–1850",
			"title": "The height of the Aro network",
			"text": "Aro settlements such as Ndikelionwu, Ajalli and Arondizuogu become small states of their own. Aro traders reach north toward the Igala and Idoma country. After Britain bans the slave trade in 1807, palm oil grows alongside it. Commanders such as Izuogu Mgbokpo and Iheme lead Aro and Abam forces to conquer Ikpa Ora and found Arondizuogu; Okoro Idozuka widens it by war early in the 19th century. Aro soldiers found villages in Ozizza and help build Afikpo, Amasiri and Izombe. Baikie hears in the 1850s that Arochukwu is nearly three times the size of Aboh, its smiths famous for swords and spears, and that people say “God lives there.”",
