		{
			"year": 1901,
			"yearLabel": "1901–1902",
			"title": "The Anglo-Aro war",
			"text": "From the 1890s the Royal Niger Company and the Niger Coast Protectorate collide with Aro trade. Sir Ralph Moor plans the attack from 1899. In November 1901 Aro forces under Okoro Toti sack Obegu, a British ally, and some 400 people die. On 28 November Lt-Col Montanaro marches with 87 officers, 1,550 soldiers and 2,100 carriers on four lines from Oguta, Akwete, Unwana and Itu; Arochukwu falls on 28 December after four days' fighting, and the oracle is said to be blown up. The last big battle is at Bende in spring 1902. Okoro Toti is hanged; Aro fighters keep up the war at Afikpo (1902–1903) and Ezza (1905).",
